     "head": "RATIONAL NUMBERS 1 1.1 Introduction In Mathematics, we frequently come across simple equations to be solved. For example, the equation x + 2 = 13 (1) is solved when x = 11, because this value of x satisfies the given equation. The solution 11 is a natural number. On the other hand, for the equation x + 5 = 5 (2) the solution gives the whole number 0 (zero). If we consider only natural numbers, equation (2) cannot be solved. To solve equations like (2), we added the number zero to the collection of natural numbers and obtained the whole numbers. Even whole numbers will not be sufficient to solve equations of type x + 18 = 5 (3) Do you see ‘why’? We require the number –13 which is not a whole number. This led us to think of integers, (positive and negative). Note that the positive integers correspond to natural numbers. One may think that we have enough numbers to solve all simple equation"
